                              2. Component action and the two sharp clocks
Lemma 2 (first-image components). If g = Mn (f ), then the off-diagonal values {g(i) : g(i) ̸= i} are
distinct. Its functional digraph therefore has only directed-cycle and loop-rooted-path components. On
these components Mn acts exactly as in Theorem 1(i), components never merge, and the recurrent-path
criterion in part (ii) holds.
Proof. Whenever g(i) ̸= i, its value is the first position of symbol i in f . Distinct symbols have distinct
first positions. Hence every vertex has at most one incoming nonloop edge. A nonloop cycle can support
no attached vertex, while a loop can support at most one chain, proving the component classification.
    Write a path as P = (p0 , . . . , ps−1 ), so g(p0 ) = p0 and g(pj ) = pj−1 for j ≥ 1. In the word g, symbol
p0 occurs at positions p0 , p1 , symbol pj for 1 ≤ j ≤ s − 2 occurs at pj+1 , and ps−1 is absent. If p0 > p1 ,
the least occurrence of p0 is p1 and the entire path reverses. If p0 < p1 , the root remains a singleton
and the other labels form the reversed path. A cycle has exactly one occurrence of every symbol, so its
orientation is inverted. All relevant positions and symbols lie in the original component, including the
identity default at an absent leaf; no merge is possible.
    For s > 1, the path returns after reversal precisely when its new root also has the descending com-
parison, namely ps−1 > ps−2 . If the first comparison fails, a split occurs immediately. If only the last
comparison fails, one reversal is followed by a split. Since splits cannot be undone, these paths are not
recurrent. This proves the criterion.                                                                        □
                                          MINIMUM INVERSE-POSITION FEEDBACK                                      3


Lemma 3 (sharp path clock). For a path P of size s, its tail D(P ) satisfies

(11)                                                  D(P ) ≤ 2s − 2.

For s ≥ 2, equality holds exactly for the strictly decreasing order p0 > p1 > · · · > ps−1 .

Proof. Induct on s. A recurrent path has tail zero. If p0 < p1 , one update splits off p0 and leaves a path
of size s − 1, so induction gives D(P ) ≤ 1 + 2(s − 1) − 2 = 2s − 3. It remains to take p0 > p1 with P
nonrecurrent. Lemma 2 then gives ps−1 < ps−2 : one update reverses the path, the next splits off ps−1 ,
and the surviving path is the original prefix P − = (p0 , . . . , ps−2 ). Therefore

                                              D(P ) = 2 + D(P − ) ≤ 2s − 2.

Equality is equivalent by induction to strict decrease of P − ; the last displayed endpoint inequality then
extends the decrease through ps−1 . Conversely, the same recurrence proves equality for every strictly
decreasing path.                                                                                         □

Proof of Theorem 1(i). Components update in parallel, so a state’s tail is the maximum of its path tails.
Lemma 3 gives the global component bound. Every g = Mn (f ) contains the coordinate value zero,
because the symbol f (0) has first occurrence zero. The unique full-label path attaining 2n − 2 has order
(n − 1, . . . , 0), and its coordinate values are {1, . . . , n − 1}; it cannot belong to Yn . Any other full-label
path has integer tail at most 2n − 3, while a path on at most n − 1 labels has tail at most 2n − 4. Thus
the second maximum in (4) is at most 2n − 3.
   For n ≥ 2, let wn (j) = j + 1 for 0 ≤ j ≤ n − 2 and wn (n − 1) = 1; in word notation this is
wn = (1, 2, . . . , n − 1, 1), and it maps to the increasing path (0, 1, . . . , n − 1). The path recursion in the
previous proof gives tail 2n − 3 for this image. Since it is transient, wn has tail 2n − 2. Conversely, every
source enters Yn after one update, so its tail is at most 1 + (2n − 3). At n = 1 the sole state is fixed. The
component assertions were proved in Lemma 2.                                                                     □


                              3. Recurrent species, fixed iterates, and zeta
Proof of Theorem 1(ii)–(iii). On a prescribed s-set, directed cycles contribute (s − 1)! for s ≥ 2. There is
one singleton. Recurrent paths contribute none at s = 2 and two at s = 3, since the middle position must
contain the smallest label. For s ≥ 4, the two endpoint inequalities concern disjoint pairs of positions.
Swapping either pair is a free involution on the s! linear orders, so exactly s!/4 orders satisfy both. This
proves (5).
   A recurrent state is an unordered labelled set of connected recurrent components. The labelled-set
formula gives
